    <semantic:process id="_15a3061a-1db1-46cb-9181-98fbd86275e1" name="Page 1" triso:readOnly="false" triso:collectEnds="true" isClosed="false">
        <semantic:dataObject id="_1e9ccf72-8b61-4ce1-9005-b7a88a4f00ef" name="Streaming plan" triso:readOnly="false" itemSubjectRef="_triso-default-bpmnItemDefinition-string_id" triso:displayName="Streaming plan" isCollection="false"/>
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_783bff5c-af80-47e5-a7eb-430f04129605">
            <semantic:text>In a streaming subscription upgrade scenario, ask: What starts the flow, where can it split, how does it rejoin, and what ends it? Name tasks verb-object and avoid crossing lines. If a decision is needed, pick the gateway that matches the semantics. If data crosses pools, use a message flow, not a sequence flow. Keep one clear idea per diagram. Here's the one-sentence way to remember it: make it obvious, and test it with token animation if your tool supports it. Share with a teammate who mixes this up.</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_7baa55d3-e505-48cc-85dc-355b82126fc0">
            <semantic:text>Define Flow objects overview in BPMN terms.
Identify when to apply Flow objects overview.
Model a 4-6 step example using Flow objects overview correctly.</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_2c8bf179-131f-44c9-b06e-a3ecbe30b84f">
            <semantic:text>Parallel Gateway “Splitting”</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
    </semantic:process>
